% Decisions of the Medicare Appeals Council on items other than power
% mobility devices — a bed, glucose test strips and glucose testing, a
% nebulizer drug refill, power wheelchair options — run through the whole
% modelled DMEPOS coverage (dmepos_all.le: every policy program of this
% directory, with dmepos.le's orders, delivery, benefit category, continued
% need and refills), so that a claim of any modelled policy meets its codes
% and a claim of none is seen to be outside the model's scope.
%
% One scenario per decision, named after its text file in sources/council/
% ("scenario ch is, as stated in Council decision ch:"), written as
% council_pmd.le writes the power mobility decisions: each fact quotes its
% passage ("confer \"...\""), the treating practitioner's findings say
% "according to the treating practitioner", the Council's "according to the
% Council" with the reason it gave. The Council's outcome is NOT a fact: it
% is in the comment above each scenario; the expectation is what the model
% derives, and where it differs the comment says DISAGREES and why — a
% criterion of the policy the Council applied that the current LCD or this
% model does not have (Policy Article A19846's exclusion of the power seat
% elevation feature, which NCD 280.16 and LCD L33792 have since covered
% conditionally; the 7-day and 5-day refill timing of the old Program
% Integrity Manual, replaced by the 30-day and 10-day rule of 42 CFR
% 410.38); a fact the decision does not state (a residence, an order's
% elements, the date an order reached the supplier, a diagnosis code, a
% quantity); a criterion of another benefit (the clinical laboratory
% benefit, whose glucose tests are laboratory services, not DMEPOS).
%
% Codes: the decisions cite the codes of their day; a code since replaced is
% transcribed as the current one and the comment says so (E2300, the power
% seat elevation system, is now E2298); a code no policy of the model decides
% (A9270, K0108, the laboratory codes 82962, 82947, 87088, 87086) is
% transcribed as billed, so that the claim is outside the model's scope and
% the query fails at applicability. Where a decision names a diagnosis but
% no code (type 1 diabetes, COPD) the ICD-10 code of the Policy Article's
% list for that diagnosis is assumed and the comment says so. Facts the
% vocabulary cannot express are listed in each comment as candidate
% templates. The fourth query, "dme", asks the DME definition of dmepos.le,
% the criterion three of these decisions turned on.

the target language is: prolog.
scenario facts require provenance.

the knowledge base council other includes these resources:
    dmepos_all.

the knowledge base council other includes:

% Where each cited decision is published, and a copy of its text (sources/).
