the text of Council decision m 12 1359 is at "sources/council/m-12-1359.txt".

query pay is:
    which claim is payable.

query rn is:
    which claim is reasonable and necessary.

query stage is:
    the query fails at which section.

query dme is:
    which item is durable medical equipment.

% C.H. (28 October 2009, own-motion review). An adjustable bed ("Twin
% Englander Adjustable Bed") and a foam mattress bought as a set by the
% beneficiary at a mattress outlet on 11 January 2008 on a physician's
% prescription for a "power bed" (psoriatic arthritis, difficulty with
% mobility and sleep), claimed as one item under A9270 (non-covered item or
% service); the ALJ had found it DME and medically necessary.
% Council: not covered — the bed and mattress do not meet the definition of
% DME (42 CFR 414.202): beds are comfort or convenience items "not considered
% primarily medical in nature" (NCD 280.1's reference list, "Beds – Lounges
% (power or manual)") and are typically used in the absence of illness;
% medical necessity for one beneficiary cannot make an item DME; and nothing
% shows a hospital bed prescribed per NCD 280.7's criteria. Section 1879 does
% not apply; the beneficiary is liable.
% Model: not covered — agrees. A9270 belongs to no modelled policy, so the
% claim is outside the model's scope (the query fails at applicability) and
% not payable; of the four DME characteristics only the two the ALJ found
% (withstands repeated use, appropriate for the home) are stated, the two
% the Council denied are not, so the bed is not durable medical equipment by
% the dmepos.le definition either. The hospital bed criteria of
% hospital_beds.le are never reached, the item not being coded as a hospital
% bed; the Council's footnote that nothing shows a hospital bed prescribed
% per NCD 280.7 is recorded as its rejection of the positioning finding
% (criteria 1-2 of a fixed height bed). The prescription's date is garbled
% in the text ("January 211, 2008") and is not transcribed.
scenario ch is, as stated in Council decision ch:
    claim 1 is for the adjustable bed,
        confer "an adjustable bed (HCPCS code A9270) purchased by the beneficiary from Mattressmax Factory Outlet on January 11, 2008".
    the HCPCS code of the adjustable bed is "A9270",
        confer "coding it under HCPCS code A9270 (non-covered item or service)".
    the adjustable bed is furnished to CH,
        confer "along with a foam mattress, at Mattressmax Factory Outlet".
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2008-01-11,
        confer "purchased by the beneficiary on January 11, 2008".
    the adjustable bed can withstand repeated use,
        confer "The item at issue can withstand repeated use".
    the adjustable bed is appropriate for use in the home,
        confer "is appropriate for use in the home (it is a bed)".
    the medical record of CH documents psoriatic arthritis,
        confer "The beneficiary has psoriatic arthritis, degenerative disc disease in the lumbar spine, and severe joint pain".
    the finding positioning infeasible in ordinary bed about CH is unsupported,
        according to the Council, because "there is no evidence that the adjustable bed was a hospital bed prescribed consistent with the criteria of NCD 280.7",
        confer "There is no evidence that the adjustable bed at issue in this case was a hospital bed prescribed consistent with the criteria".
    the order for the adjustable bed is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "Dr. *** signed a prescription for an adjustable bed".
    the order for the adjustable bed includes description of the item,
        confer "Please let [the] patient have power bed".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].
    dme expects answers [].

% M-10-1843, R.J. (31 December 2010). 24 units of blood glucose test strips
% (A4253, 50 strips a unit: 1,200 strips) furnished by RX Solutions for the
% period of service 25 July to 22 October 2008 to a type 1 diabetic of 40
% years on an insulin pump, ordered to test 10-12 times a day for
% hypoglycemia unawareness and a fluctuating glycemic trend; the QIC allowed
% 14 units on the logs (7 tests a day) and denied 10; order of 23 July 2008
% signed 24 July; office visit of 10 July 2008 ("poor glycemic control"); the
% wife's refill call of 8 July 2008; May 2008 logs averaging 12 tests a day
% over continuation sheets, signed and dated 3 June 2008.
% Council: covered — the high-utilization criteria (a)-(f) of LCD L196 are
% met: the monitor criteria, the order on file, the supply nearly exhausted
% (the refill call), the order's frequency and its reason, the visit within
% six months, and the logs corroborating the frequency.
% Model: 1,200 strips for 3 months exceed the 300 of an insulin-treated
% beneficiary, so the high-utilization criteria of the current LCD L33822
% apply: the visit within six months before the order (13 days) is met; but
% the claim is a refill, and criterion (c) — for continued dispensing "the
% treating practitioner must verify adherence to the high utilization
% testing regimen" every six months — is a fact the decision does not state:
% L196 put the corroboration of the testing frequency on the record or the
% supplier's records (the logs, criterion (f), which the current Policy
% Article keeps as a documentation requirement, met here), not on the
% practitioner. DISAGREES on that criterion alone: with "the treating
% practitioner verified adherence of RJ to the high utilization testing
% regimen on 2008-07-23" the claim is reasonable and necessary. The
% diagnosis code is not stated: E10.9 (type 1 diabetes mellitus) is assumed.
% The three-month supply is the period of service; the home is the "home
% blood glucose monitor" the Council found him to need. Not payable also
% because the order's other elements, the date the order reached the
% supplier, the delivery and the expected end of the previous supply are not
% stated.
scenario rj_test_strips is, as stated in Council decision rj test strips:
    claim 1 is for the test strips,
        confer "the supplier’s claim for Medicare coverage of 24 units of blood glucose test strips (HCPCS code A4253)".
    the HCPCS code of the test strips is "A4253",
        confer "24 units of blood glucose test strips (HCPCS code A4253)".
    the test strips is furnished to RJ,
        confer "which it furnished to the beneficiary for the period of service occurring from July 25, 2008, through October 22, 2008".
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2008-07-25,
        confer "for the period of service occurring from July 25, 2008, through October 22, 2008".
    claim 1 is a refill,
        confer "the beneficiary’s wife called about getting a refill on his test strips on July 8, 2008".
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 24,
        confer "24 units of blood glucose test strips (HCPCS code A4253)".
    claim 1 is for a supply of 3 months,
        confer "for the period of service occurring from July 25, 2008, through October 22, 2008".
    the diagnosis code of RJ is "E10.9",
        confer "longstanding history of type 1 diabetes mellitus, currently treating with insulin pump".
    RJ is currently being treated with insulin,
        confer "currently treating with insulin pump".
    the residence of RJ is private residence,
        confer "his need for a home blood glucose monitor".
    the finding trained on blood glucose monitor about RJ is established,
        according to the treating practitioner,
        confer "which reflects that the beneficiary is to test 10 to 12 times per day".
    the order for the test strips is dated 2008-07-23,
        confer "The record contains an order dated July 23, 2008, and signed by the treating physician on July 24, 2008".
    the order for the test strips is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "The record contains an order dated July 23, 2008, and signed by the treating physician on July 24, 2008".
    the order for the test strips includes description of the item,
        confer "This order specifically indicates that the beneficiary is to test at a frequency that would exceed the utilization guidelines".
    the treating practitioner had an in-person or telehealth visit with RJ to evaluate diabetes control on 2008-07-10,
        confer "The record contains a physician’s note documenting a visit with the beneficiary on July 10, 2008".
    the testing frequency of RJ is corroborated by testing log,
        confer "The May 2008, test logs reflect that the beneficiary performed an average of".
    the medical record documents continued need for the test strips on 2008-07-23,
        confer "The record contains an order dated July 23, 2008".
    the record documents continued use of the test strips on 2008-06-03,
        confer "each sheet was in fact signed by the beneficiary and dated June 3, 2008".
    the beneficiary affirmed the need for the refill of the test strips on 2008-07-08,
        confer "the beneficiary’s wife called about getting a refill on his test strips on July 8, 2008".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at question"].

% M-11-545, Amigo Home Health Agency (24 April 2012). Blood glucose tests
% (CPT 82962, glucose by an FDA-cleared home monitoring device) performed by
% the agency's nurses on its home health patients — 62 sampled beneficiaries,
% diabetics, many on insulin, each with a home-use monitor of their own —
% between September 2004 and June 2008 under standing orders on the 60-day
% home health plans of care (sliding-scale insulin), and billed to Part B as
% laboratory services beside the Part A home health payment; an extrapolated
% overpayment. One beneficiary stands for the sample.
% Council: not covered — the tests were performed under standing orders; the
% results reached the physician weekly, only aberrant readings at once, so
% the physician did not promptly use each result in the beneficiary's
% management (42 CFR 410.32, PM AB-00-108, NCD 190.20); and a beneficiary who
% maintains a home-use monitor has the service within the home health
% benefit.
% Model: not covered — agrees on the outcome only: 82962 is a laboratory
% code no policy of the model decides, so the claim is outside its scope
% (the query fails at applicability). The criteria the Council applied are
% those of the clinical laboratory benefit, not of the DME benefit the
% glucose monitor policy models (whose test strips and lancets the same
% beneficiaries received as DME). Candidate templates: "*a test* was
% performed under a standing order on the plan of care of *a beneficiary*",
% "the result of *a test* was reported to the treating practitioner on *a
% date*", "the treating practitioner used the result of *a test* in the
% management of *a beneficiary*", "*a beneficiary* maintains a home-use
% glucose monitoring device", "*a beneficiary* is under a home health plan
% of care on *a date*".
scenario m_11_545 is, as stated in Council decision m 11 545:
    claim 1 is for the blood glucose test,
        confer "code 82962 (Glucose, blood by glucose monitoring devices(s) cleared by the FDA specifically for home use)".
    the HCPCS code of the blood glucose test is "82962",
        confer "code 82962 (Glucose, blood by glucose monitoring devices(s) cleared by the FDA specifically for home use)".
    the blood glucose test is furnished to the beneficiary,
        confer "blood glucose testing services provided to beneficiaries between September 1, 2004 and June 30, 2008".
    the medical record of the beneficiary documents diabetes,
        confer "the beneficiaries generally had diabetes and many were on insulin".
    the beneficiary is currently being treated with insulin,
        confer "a standing order, renewed every 60 days, for regular blood glucose testing and a sliding scale of insulin administration depending upon the glucose reading".
    the residence of the beneficiary is private residence,
        confer "the appellant performed frequent blood glucose monitoring on home health patients".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].

% M-11-1002, E.K. (20 December 2012). Laboratory tests ordered by the
% transplant physicians after a kidney transplant of 20 October 2009 and
% performed near home: a blood glucose test (82947) on 30 November 2009, a
% urine culture (87088) and a urine colony count (87086) on 30 December 2009
% and 18 January 2010; the orders carried ICD-9 V42.0 (kidney replaced by
% transplant) and V58.61 (long-term anticoagulants), and the physician's
% redetermination request added 599.0 (urinary tract infection) and 599.7
% (hematuria).
% Council: covered — 599.0 and 599.7 are listed for urine cultures in the
% NCD 190.12 coding manual and 599.0 for blood glucose testing in the NCD
% 190.20 manual, the NCD indicating urine cultures for transplant recipients
% on immunosuppressants; the contractor, the QIC and the ALJ erred in
% ignoring the added codes.
% Model: not covered — DISAGREES: 82947, 87088 and 87086 are clinical
% laboratory codes no policy of the model decides (the queries fail at
% applicability), and the glucose monitor policy reads a diagnosis code
% against the Policy Article's diabetes list, which the ICD-9 codes of 2009
% are not on (the codes are transcribed as the decision gives them). The
% laboratory NCDs and their coding manuals are outside the model. Candidate
% templates: "ICD9 code *a code* supports *a test* under NCD *an NCD*", "*a
% test* is a clinical diagnostic laboratory test", "*a beneficiary* is a
% renal transplant recipient on immunosuppressive therapy".
scenario m-11-1002 is, as stated in Council decision m 11 1002:
    claim 1 is for the blood glucose test,
        confer "These laboratory tests included a blood glucose test (82947), a urine culture (87088)".
    the HCPCS code of the blood glucose test is "82947",
        confer "a blood glucose test (82947)".
    the blood glucose test is furnished to EK,
        confer "Medicare covers the blood glucose testing (82947) provided to the beneficiary on November 30, 2009".
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2009-11-30,
        confer "the blood glucose testing (82947) provided to the beneficiary on November 30, 2009".
    claim 2 is for the urine culture,
        confer "a urine culture (87088)".
    the HCPCS code of the urine culture is "87088",
        confer "a urine culture (87088)".
    the urine culture is furnished to EK,
        confer "the urine culture (87088) and urine colony count (87086) testing provided to the beneficiary on December 30, 2009 and January 18, 2010".
    the date of service of claim 2 is 2009-12-30,
        confer "the urine culture (87088) and urine colony count (87086) testing provided to the beneficiary on December 30, 2009 and January 18, 2010".
    claim 3 is for the urine colony count,
        confer "a colony count of bacteria in the urine (87086)".
    the HCPCS code of the urine colony count is "87086",
        confer "a colony count of bacteria in the urine (87086)".
    the urine colony count is furnished to EK,
        confer "the urine culture (87088) and urine colony count (87086) testing provided to the beneficiary on December 30, 2009 and January 18, 2010".
    the date of service of claim 3 is 2010-01-18,
        confer "the urine culture (87088) and urine colony count (87086) testing provided to the beneficiary on December 30, 2009 and January 18, 2010".
    the diagnosis code of EK is "V42.0",
        confer "The orders contained diagnostic codes of v42.0 (kidney replaced by transplant) and v58.61 (long term use of anti-coagulants)".
    the diagnosis code of EK is "599.0",
        confer "listed the additional diagnostic codes for urinary tract infection (599.0) and hematuria (599.7)".
    the diagnosis code of EK is "599.7",
        confer "listed the additional diagnostic codes for urinary tract infection (599.0) and hematuria (599.7)".
    the medical record of EK documents systemic lupus erythematosus,
        confer "The beneficiary has also been diagnosed with systemic lupus erythematosus".
    the order for the blood glucose test is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "wrote orders so that the appellant could have blood tests and laboratory analysis performed closer to home".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].

% M-12-1456, AeroCare Holdings (3 August 2012). A refill of formoterol
% fumarate inhalation solution (J7606, the code for formoterol; the decision
% names none) with date of service 13 January 2011 for a beneficiary with
% COPD: prescription of 16 July 2010 for a one-month supply refillable for 12
% months; the supplier's call to the wife on 13 January 2011 confirming the
% need (eight doses left, twice daily), shipment the same day, delivery on 17
% January 2011.
% Council: covered — under LCD L5007 (MPIM ch. 4 §4.26.1 as then written)
% the call log is a refill request (no signed request is needed), contact on
% the shipping day is within "no sooner than approximately 7 days prior",
% delivery on 17 January is within 5 days of the end of the supply (16 or 17
% January), and the record has a valid prescription and proof of delivery.
% Model: reasonable and necessary — agrees: formoterol for COPD (the
% diagnosis code is not stated: J44.9, a Group 8 code of the Policy Article,
% is assumed), no concurrent long-acting beta agonist, within the 62 units a
% month (the quantity billed is not stated: 60 units, the month of
% twice-daily 20 mcg doses the decision describes, is taken). The refill
% rule of the current 42 CFR 410.38 (dmepos.le), which replaced the 7-day
% and 5-day rule the Council applied — the affirmative response within 30
% days before the expected end of the supply, the date of service within 10
% days before it — is met with the end taken as 16 January 2011, the earlier
% of the two days the Council computed. Not payable: the residence (the
% benefit category needs the home), the prescription's other elements and
% the date it reached the supplier are not stated.
scenario aerocare_holdings is, as stated in Council decision aerocare holdings:
    claim 1 is for the formoterol,
        confer "coverage for a formoterol fumarate inhalation solution (formoterol) refill provided to the beneficiary with date of service January 13, 2011".
    the HCPCS code of the formoterol is "J7606",
        confer "a formoterol fumarate inhalation solution (formoterol) refill".
    the formoterol is furnished to the beneficiary,
        confer "refill provided to the beneficiary with date of service January 13, 2011".
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2011-01-13,
        confer "refill provided to the beneficiary with date of service January 13, 2011".
    claim 1 is a refill,
        confer "confirmed that the beneficiary required a formoterol refill".
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 60,
        confer "a dosage of formoterol was administered twice daily".
    claim 1 is for a supply of 1 months,
        confer "a physician prescribed to the beneficiary a one month supply of formoterol".
    the diagnosis code of the beneficiary is "J44.9",
        confer "The beneficiary is diagnosed with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)".
    the order for the formoterol is dated 2010-07-16,
        confer "On July 16, 2010, a physician prescribed to the beneficiary a one month supply of formoterol and other inhalation solutions refillable up to 12 months".
    the order for the formoterol is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "the record includes a valid prescription for the refill".
    the order for the formoterol includes order date,
        confer "On July 16, 2010, a physician prescribed to the beneficiary a one month supply of formoterol".
    the order for the formoterol includes description of the item,
        confer "a one month supply of formoterol".
    the order for the formoterol includes quantity to be dispensed,
        confer "a one month supply of formoterol".
    the current supply of the formoterol is expected to end on 2011-01-16,
        confer "the beneficiary would have exhausted his current supply of formoterol on January 16, 2011, or January 17, 2011".
    the beneficiary affirmed the need for the refill of the formoterol on 2011-01-13,
        confer "On January 13, 2011, the appellant contacted the beneficiary’s wife and confirmed that the beneficiary required a formoterol refill".
    the medical record documents continued need for the formoterol on 2010-07-16,
        confer "a one month supply of formoterol and other inhalation solutions refillable up to 12 months".
    the record documents continued use of the formoterol on 2011-01-13,
        confer "the current product’s usage frequency and remaining dosages".
    the formoterol was delivered on 2011-01-17,
        confer "it was delivered on January 17, 2011".
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the formoterol,
        confer "a valid proof of delivery".
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].

% M-11-2045, National Seating & Mobility (20 September 2011, own-motion
% review). Four accessories of a Group 4 power wheelchair (K0870, paid by the
% contractor with its battery E2363 and never in dispute) furnished 6 January
% 2010 to a man with inclusion body myositis: a power seat elevation system
% (then E2300, now E2298), the electronics connecting it to the controller
% (E2310), an expandable controller (E2377) and its harness (E2313).
% Council: E2300 and E2310 not covered — under Policy Article A19846 a power
% seat elevation feature is "not primarily medical in nature", so it is not
% DME whatever its necessity, and a connection whose sole function is the
% seat elevation is denied with it; section 1879 does not apply, an ABN
% having been signed. E2377 and E2313: the ALJ's favorable findings adopted,
% CMS having raised no objection.
% Model: none of the four reasonable and necessary. E2298 and E2310: agrees
% on the outcome, on different grounds — the current LCD L33792 (since NCD
% 280.16 of 2023) covers a seat elevation system on a Group 2 power-option
% or Group 3 base whose beneficiary meets the NCD's criteria; the base here
% is a Group 4 chair, which no rule of pmd.le covers ("added capabilities
% that are not needed for use in the home", and K0870 is outside pmd.le's
% codes: the base claim fails at applicability), and no NCD 280.16 finding
% was made. E2377 and E2313: DISAGREES — every option needs a covered base,
% and the model cannot take the contractor's payment of the K0870 as its
% coverage; nor, under the current Article A52504, is an expandable
% controller covered without an alternative drive control interface on the
% chair, which none of the four is. The stage of the whole is the base's.
% Candidate templates: "*a device* was found covered by the contractor on *a
% date*", "the beneficiary signed an advance beneficiary notice for *an item*
% on *a date*".
